#!/usr/bin/ucode -S
// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
// Print the interface each configured instance owns, one name per line.
//
// "Owns" is load_settings()' answer and nothing else: the raw `interface`
// option run through validate_interface, falling back to the default name
// when it is refused. That is the name every stamped routing/firewall object
// carries, so it is the name anything reasoning about ownership has to use.
//
// It is answered HERE, against the same cursor the runtime reads through,
// rather than reconstructed by a caller from `uci get` output, because a uci
// value does not survive that trip as the thing the runtime saw:
//
//   * uci permits a newline INSIDE a value. Captured from `uci get` it
//     becomes two lines, and the first of them looks like a perfectly good
//     interface name; read through the cursor it is one string,
//     validate_interface refuses it, and the instance owns the default name.
//   * a one-item list prints as a bare word through `uci get` and arrives as
//     an array at the cursor, where it is not a string and is refused the
//     same way.
//
// In both cases a reconstructed answer names an interface that owns nothing
// and leaves the real owner off the list. The uci-defaults migration decides
// what is an orphan by this list, and an owner missing from it means a LIVE
// instance's stamped objects are deleted — its IPv6 prohibit among them,
// which is a leak.
//
// Exits non-zero if the configuration cannot be read, so a caller can tell
// "could not find out" from "nothing is configured" — the two must never be
// the same answer to anything that deletes. Any CLI arguments are ignored.
//
// The output is FRAMED: the names, then a final "# owners <n>" line. That
// line is what lets a caller verify it received the whole answer and nothing
// else, structurally, without knowing what an interface name looks like —
// a truncated run loses the frame, and anything else reaching this stdout
// breaks the count. The rule for what a name IS stays in validate_interface,
// where it is stated once; a caller that re-derived it in order to sanity
// check this output would be the second definition all over again, agreeing
// today and drifting silently later.

'use strict';

import { cursor } from 'uci';
const _common = require('protonvpn.common');

let uci = cursor();
let names = _common.list_instances(uci);
for (let name in names)
	print(_common.load_settings(uci, name).interface, '\n');
print('# owners ', length(names), '\n');
